"""Shared workflows. Service/product examples are added by scripts/guides.py."""
BOOKS=[]
def book(letter,slug,title,track,goal,free,paid,steps,example,fixes,next_books):
 BOOKS.append(dict(letter=letter,path=f'playbooks/playbook-{letter.lower()}-{slug}.html',title=title,track=track,goal=goal,free=free,paid=paid,steps=steps,example=example,fixes=fixes,next=next_books))
def step(title,action,prompt,output,check):return dict(title=title,action=action,prompt=prompt,output=output,check=check)
FREE=['Start with a free writing tool and one free editor: Docs, Sheets, Slides, Canva or a video editor. Use your own files or assets you have permission to use.','Finish one practice output before buying anything. Free-tier limits can change. Save your work and test a free export first.']
PAID=['Paid AI, design or research tools are optional. Pay for a specific problem, such as a longer source file or repeated editing task.','Read the current plan, country support and credit cost in Tools & costs. Budget for retries. A subscription is not a fixed number of finished deliverables or sales.']
FIX=[('I do not know what to make','Open Choose my offer. Pick one small service or product, then use the matching example on each step. Make the smallest version you can check.'),('AI gives a vague answer','Paste the actual facts and required output. Say which sentence or step is unclear. Ask it to show one filled example and exact next action.'),('I finished it but nobody bought','Review who saw it, what they asked and where they stopped. Ask a relevant person to try the preview. Change one thing, then test again. Do not treat views as sales.')]
EX={'title':'See what finished work looks like','body':'This reference uses made-up practice facts. It shows the files and words someone could send to a client. You are learning the process, not choosing that business as your only customer type. Select your offer above to see the closest example.', 'asset':'service-examples.html'}
PX={'title':'A complete small download you can inspect','body':'The Freelancer Project Starter is an example of a finished workbook. It helps a freelancer organize a project. It does not find clients for them. Open it, complete its first task and notice how instructions, a filled example and blank pages work together. Use the product examples above for your chosen idea.','asset':'freelancer-project-starter.html'}
book('A','land-first-client','Find and pitch your first service clients','service','Choose one service, make a small sample and find suitable people to show it to. Use any appropriate research channel, not just Maps.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Choose the work you will sell','Open Choose my offer. Pick one of ten services, take the short interview, or choose “My own service” without typing anything. Write a sentence: “I help [buyer] with [specific work]. You receive [files] by [agreed date].” Choose a buyer type you understand. List three types, pick one with an observable problem and reachable business contact. You can change the buyer type later.','Help me define a small service using [chosen service], [skills I can demonstrate], [hours available] and [buyer types I understand]. Ask up to five questions if needed. Recommend one buyer type and a bounded package. Give exact files, required client inputs, what is excluded, practice time and one quality check. Write a plain offer sentence. No promised income, bookings or client results.','One offer sentence and a list of what is included and excluded.','You can explain what the buyer gets and make a practice version yourself.'),
 step('Find ten suitable businesses','Choose one channel below. Search for a business type and visible problem relevant to your service. Read the page or post yourself. Add Business, Source link, Checked date, What I noticed, Possible help, Contact route and Unknowns to your tracker. Start with explicit requests for help. A weak website or quiet account does not prove buying interest.','Help me prepare a research-agent prompt. My service: [service or unknown]. Buyer type: [type or unknown]. Location: [city/country or remote]. Preferred channels: [Instagram/Facebook Groups/LinkedIn/Maps/websites]. What I already know: [facts and source links]. Ask up to five useful questions to fill gaps. Then write a detailed prompt for Manus or Z.ai to find ten suitable businesses, with source URLs, checked dates, visible observations, public business contact routes and unknowns. No guessed contacts, private personal data, invented budgets, automated messaging or login bypass. Require a small first batch and manual verification.','Ten checked rows, or an honest smaller list with the reason.','You opened every source. Each observation is specific and relevant to your offer.'),
 step('Make a small sample they can judge','A sample is a small piece of the work you would sell. A copywriter can rewrite one headline. A designer can make one carousel. A website seller can build one demo page. Use the recipe for your chosen service below. Label it “practice demo” or “unpaid sample”. Use public facts, then ask permission before publishing someone’s brand or work.','Create one small [chosen service] sample using only [approved or public facts]. Buyer: [type]. Problem: [visible issue]. Show what changed and why in three plain bullets. First ask for missing essential inputs. Keep it small enough to review in two minutes. Give the files to create and a check list. No invented testimonial, performance metric or claim that this was commissioned work.','One checked PDF, image, page, clip or Sheet plus a short explanation.','The sample matches the chosen service and shows actual work, not a promise.'),
 step('Ask if they want to see the sample','Choose one verified business and one appropriate contact route. Write one observation, explain your small sample and ask permission to share it. Use the channel examples below. Send manually. Record the date and response. A group reply should help with the question before mentioning your service.','Write three versions of a permission message for [Instagram DM / Facebook group reply / LinkedIn / business email / phone]. Business: [name]. Source: [URL]. Verified observation: [fact]. My service: [service]. Small sample: [what I made]. Ask one easy question. Keep the DM under 65 words. For a group, answer the original question before asking whether they want a sample. No invented experience, pressure, insulting their current work or guaranteed results.','A few relevant messages and a tracker row for each one sent.','No brackets remain. The message fits the channel and the person’s visible needs.'),
 step('Understand the reply before quoting','If they say yes, send the promised sample first. Ask what they need, what they already have, who approves it, when it is needed and what budget they have in mind. If there is no reply, use at most one relevant check-in after several days where rules and context permit, then stop. Stop after a refusal.','Here is the actual conversation [messages]. Help me reply in plain language. First deliver [promised sample]. Ask only the next two questions needed to define the work. Identify missing inputs, deadline, approver and budget. If there is no response, draft one respectful check-in with an easy exit. Do not treat silence as interest or consent. Do not send anything.','A clear request for work, or a closed conversation with no pressure.','You know the files, deadline, approver and unresolved questions before making a quote.'),
 step('Agree the price, make the work and deliver','Estimate research, making, checking, revisions and admin time. Add actual tool costs and a margin you can defend. Confirm the amount, currency, files, inputs, dates, revision limit, payment schedule and ongoing costs in writing. Payment terms are agreed with the client. Then use F to produce and check the work. Ask permission before using feedback or client work in public.','Write a plain project agreement from these agreed facts [facts]. Include exact deliverables, client inputs, deadline, revision limit, exclusions, price/currency, payment milestones, third-party costs and who owns accounts/files. Mark unresolved items as questions. Add a delivery email and approval request. This is a business draft to review, not a claim of legal completeness. No guaranteed business outcomes.','A written agreement, checked delivery and approval request.','Price covers the agreed work. The client knows what is included and any recurring costs.')],EX,FIX,['F','G','I'])
book('B','launch-product','Build and test a small product','product','Choose a research-backed starting idea, test the problem and make a complete first version. For physical affiliate products, use J instead of building a download.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Choose one product and check the problem','Open Choose my offer. The recommended low-cost starting point is a freelancer project-start kit, if you know freelancers you can ask for feedback. It is a recommendation about build simplicity, not a sales forecast. Pick another of ten choices or your own idea. For a download, collect five real questions and compare five existing alternatives. Ask three likely users how they do the task now.','I am considering [product] for [buyer]. Here are actual questions, alternatives and links [research]. Separate facts, guesses and unknowns. Identify one repeated task and what free alternatives already do. Ask three interview questions. Suggest the smallest useful product only after reviewing the evidence. Do not turn an AI score, views or template counts into proof of willingness to pay.','One buyer, one task and an evidence table with links and dates.','At least one likely user has seen a small preview. You can explain what your version adds.'),
 step('List exactly what the buyer will receive','Write the file list before creating it. Include a Start here page, one filled example, blank reusable pages and a final check. Explain software needed and what it does not do. For your own idea, keep the first output small enough to test in one session.','Create a product file plan for [buyer] to do [task], using [evidence]. Give exact filenames, contents, a five-minute first task, a filled fictional example, blank reusable pages and a test checklist. Explain required software and limitations. Avoid specialist claims I cannot support. The product must help someone do a task, not just read an outline.','A named file list and clear first task.','A stranger understands what they receive and how to start.'),
 step('Create it and follow your own instructions','Use the exact build recipe for your product below. Make one section or tab at a time. Replace vague instructions with a click, field or example. Use fictional example inputs and label them. Finish the blank buyer copy separately. Then give it to a tester and watch where they get stuck.','Build [section/tab] of [product] using [verified facts]. Give exact text, fields, formulas where needed, one filled fictional example and a blank buyer version. Explain every heading in simple words. List five tests including empty data, adding a new row and opening on a phone. Do not invent research, sales or results.','A complete first version and notes from a user test.','The tester reaches the stated result. Formulas, links, copy access and downloads work.'),
 step('Choose a storefront, test the price and delivery','Compare five actual alternatives by buyer, price, contents and format. Choose a test price that reflects your work and buyer feedback. Open a storefront that supports your location and payout method. Configure the real product, billing type, file delivery, support and refund terms. Use official test or preview flows before publishing. Never sell an empty file.','Compare these current alternative offers [URLs, dates, prices, contents] with my finished product [files]. Suggest three test prices with reasons, not claimed market rates. Calculate receipts using [verified platform fees]. Give a storefront setup checklist: location eligibility, billing, files, receipt, support, refund terms and test delivery. Mark anything not verified.','One configured checkout and a successful file-access test.','The billing matches the promise and a buyer can access and use the correct files.'),
 step('Publish a clear page and show the product working','Show who it helps, the task, exact contents, a readable preview, required software, limits, price and actual refund/support terms. Record one small task in the product. Do not use made-up customers or income claims. Use C or E to turn the demonstration into content.','Write a friendly product page using only [finished files], [buyer], [task], [test feedback], [price], [access instructions] and [actual refund/support terms]. Include contents, software requirements, a first-task demonstration and one purchase button. Do not invent testimonials, discounts, scarcity or earnings proof. Add a page-and-delivery check list.','A clear product page and an honest demonstration.','A new visitor knows what they buy, what it helps with and how to get started.')],PX,FIX,['C','E','I'])
book('C','faceless-funnel','Record seven useful demonstration videos','both','Make seven short videos showing real work. You can use your screen, hands and voice without showing your face.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Choose a result you can show','Finish the output first. Pick one visible change: a vague headline becomes clear, a blank planner becomes a useful week, or a real stand holds your phone. Write “Before: __. After: __.” Open both states before recording. Show your actual files or item.','My chosen offer is [offer]. I have actually made or tested [output]. Give five specific video angles with a visible before/after and one useful lesson. Use only [verified process]. For each say what must appear on screen to support the claim. No income, reach, customer or product-performance claims I cannot show.','One before/after sentence and a finished screen or product to film.','The viewer can see the result your hook describes.'),
 step('Write seven short scripts with shots','Use seven angles: one mistake, a quick tutorial, a before/after, free versus paid tools, a common question, an output check and a full short walkthrough. Each video teaches one point. Use the shot-and-timing recipe below. Read the script aloud before recording.','Create seven 25–40 second scripts for [offer], [audience] and [verified workflow]. Angles: mistake, tutorial, before/after, free/paid, FAQ, quality check, full mini walkthrough. For each give second-by-second shot, spoken line, short on-screen text and one CTA. Include actual output early. Use plain language, no fictional proof or repeated hard sell.','Seven scripts with exactly what to film for each line.','Every spoken claim has a matching shot and each video has one useful point.'),
 step('Record the reusable clips','Record five clean clips: the starting problem, the instructions or prompt, work being made, the human check and the finished output. On a computer use its screen-recording tool. On a phone use screen recording or the camera. Zoom so text reads vertically. Keep private account details out of the frame. Save clips with clear names.','Turn this script [script] into a recording checklist. Use SCREEN or OWN PRODUCT first. Say what to open, what cursor/hand action to show, approximate duration and filename. If stock is needed, give a specific Pexels search phrase and explain that it is illustrative. Generated footage must not impersonate a real customer or product test.','A folder with five labeled clips and original source files.','The output is readable and visuals are your own or permitted for the intended use.'),
 step('Edit one video, then reuse the project','Open VN or CapCut → New project → add clips → set 9:16. Put the result in the first few seconds, then show the steps that explain it. Add your voice. Add checked captions manually if the automatic feature is paid. Export 1080 × 1920 MP4. Play muted and with sound on your phone. Duplicate the project for the other scripts.','Review this timeline [seconds, shots, speech]. Suggest exact cuts to remove waiting, match words to actions and make small text readable. Keep one useful result and one next step. Give caption corrections and a final phone-preview checklist. Do not add claims beyond the footage.','One checked MP4 and a reusable edit project.','It makes sense muted, audio is clear and the screen supports the words.'),
 step('Post a small batch and record real responses','Write a caption naming the task you showed and one next action. For a service, offer the sample. For a download, show the preview or product page. For an affiliate item, disclose the relationship and use an approved link. Log post, date, qualified replies, clicks and actual purchases. Publish the first strong video, then schedule the rest at a pace you can support.','Create a seven-post plan from [finished scripts], [offer], [actual CTA and link]. Give a plain caption, one CTA and tracker fields for each. Separate views, relevant replies, clicks, purchases and available payouts. Ask what actual result would justify changing the hook or preview. Do not promise a sale from a fixed number of posts.','Seven finished videos and a posting/tracking plan.','Every post leads to the correct sample, page or eligible link.')],{'title':'Seven recording angles you can adapt','body':'These scripts show an actual process, not claimed earnings. Replace the offer and visible output with yours. The filming recipe in each step tells you what to record.','asset':'seven-video-scripts.txt'},FIX,['E','I','D'])
book('D','scale-to-2k','Improve the work using real numbers','both','Check time, costs and feedback before increasing your workload or spending on tools.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Write down actual receipts and costs','Open your weekly tracker. Record paid orders or settled commission, refunds, fees, tools, sample costs and hours. Keep pending money separate. A quote, promise, view or click is not collected revenue.','Use these actual records [receipts, fees, refunds, costs, hours]. Calculate gross receipts, net after listed costs and net per hour. Keep unpaid invoices and pending commissions separate. State missing costs and assumptions. Do not project income from a fixed conversion rate.','A weekly cost-and-time record.','All figures come from actual records or are clearly labeled planning assumptions.'),
 step('Find the slowest or weakest step','Look at the last finished project or product. Which part used the most time? Which question confused the buyer? Choose one issue to fix. For a service, reduce unclear revisions. For a download, improve the first-use instructions. For affiliate content, check product fit and link accuracy.','Review [actual time log and buyer feedback]. Identify one bottleneck in making, checking, explaining or delivering this offer. Give one change I can test without buying another tool. Explain how to compare the next result fairly. Do not assume more output is always better.','One specific improvement to test.','You know which change you made and what evidence you will compare.'),
 step('Write a checklist you can repeat','Open Docs and write the steps you actually used. Start with required inputs and end with the final test and delivery. Include time notes, filenames and what needs owner approval. Ask someone else to explain the checklist back to you.','Turn this actual workflow [steps] into a plain checklist. Include inputs, exact actions, expected file, check and approval point. Explain technical words. Keep the current tool stack. Highlight where a novice would get stuck and give a filled example.','A reusable making-and-delivery checklist.','The checklist describes work you have completed, not an imagined automation.'),
 step('Decide whether price or tools should change','Estimate all hours for the next agreed scope. Add actual costs and a buffer for a reasonable revision. If considering a subscription, compare monthly cost with realistic time saved. Do not call the most expensive plan the best. Communicate any new quote before work begins.','Compare my current offer [scope, hours, costs, price] with [proposed change]. Calculate the break-even time saved for a tool costing [amount/month] using [chosen value per hour]. State assumptions. Suggest a clear quote explanation without claiming market rates or guaranteed ROI.','A reasoned price or tool decision.','The decision reflects actual effort and does not hide ongoing costs.'),
 step('Repeat only what you can deliver well','Choose the next small batch or project based on real feedback and available time. Keep a limit on open work. For repeat services, agree recurring scope and cancellation terms separately. For products, update instructions and support. Check payout timing before counting money for a deadline.','Plan my next week from [available hours], [actual results], [open commitments] and [one improvement]. Give a realistic work limit, production tasks, buyer conversations and review time. Separate planned revenue from collected and available funds. No target is a guaranteed result.','One manageable next-week plan.','You can meet existing promises and still review quality.')],{'title':'A weekly review sheet','body':'Use real receipts and costs. Empty figures mean unknown, not zero. A planning calculation is not an earnings story.','asset':'weekly-scorecard.csv'},FIX,['A','B','I'])
book('E','carousel-leads','Make a carousel that teaches one useful step','both','Create a clear seven-slide lesson with your real example and a relevant next action. Test it with your audience.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Pick one reader and one question','Use a question a likely client or buyer actually asked. For a service, teach how to spot or fix one problem. For a product, show one task inside it. For an affiliate item, show fit, use and a limitation. Keep the lesson small.','Audience: [buyer]. Actual question: [question and source]. Offer: [chosen service/product]. Suggest five carousel angles that answer this question with a visible example. Give one sentence describing what the reader can do after seven slides. No unsupported income or platform-reach claims.','One question and one useful lesson.','The lesson fits a real buyer rather than everyone who wants money.'),
 step('Write the seven slides','Use: 1 question, 2 common mistake, 3 first action, 4 second action, 5 filled example, 6 check, 7 next step. Keep headings short. Put the exact click or prompt where needed. Do not turn every slide into a product claim.','Write seven carousel slides for [question] using [verified workflow and output]. Follow question, mistake, action one, action two, filled example, check, next step. Give heading under eight words, body under forty words and visual suggestion per slide. Use simple grammar. No fictional proof, excessive jargon or guaranteed outcomes.','Seven slide texts and image instructions.','Someone can follow the lesson without buying or asking what a term means.'),
 step('Build a simple design in Canva','Create a 1080 × 1350 design. Use one background, one accent and two fonts. Add a small slide number. Leave generous margins. Put the actual screenshot or example on slide five. Duplicate the first layout to keep the set consistent.','Give a simple Canva layout plan for these seven slides [text]. Use [colors], a readable heading/body system and one visual per slide. Explain where to place the example screenshot and how to crop without hiding the result. Use original or permitted assets.','Seven designed pages with a consistent reading order.','Text is readable on your phone and each slide has one clear point.'),
 step('Export and check the swipe order','Download all seven pages as PNGs. Open them in order on a phone. Check spelling, cropped screenshots, margin and duplicated/missing slides. Ask a tester what the lesson taught. If they cannot say, simplify before posting.','Review this slide text and order [slides]. Find missing steps, hard words, unsupported claims and a confusing transition. Give exact replacements. End with a phone-export checklist and one question to ask a tester. Do not claim carousels are universally favored by Instagram.','Seven ordered PNG files and a checked caption.','The lesson works without the caption and the files are in the correct order.'),
 step('Give one next action and learn from replies','Choose one action: save the lesson, ask for the sample, view the product preview or check an eligible item. Use one clear caption. Track saves and views separately from relevant replies and purchases. Use your own results to compare formats.','Write three caption choices for [carousel], [buyer] and [actual next step]. Use one CTA each. Include affiliate disclosure when relevant. Give a tracking plan separating saves, views, replies, clicks and sales. Avoid engagement claims or fake urgency.','A published or scheduled carousel and a response plan.','The next action matches what the post promised.')],{'title':'A seven-slide structure','body':'Use this structure to teach a decision. Replace the sample with your actual work. Slides are useful for an ordered lesson, without assuming an algorithm boost.','asset':'carousel-storyboard.txt'},FIX,['I','C','D'])
book('F','content-service','Make and deliver your chosen service','service','Follow the same client-work process for all ten services. The example and production recipe change with your selected service.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Ask for the facts and files you need','Tell the client what you are making and request the inputs for your chosen service below. “Client instructions” means the facts, files, deadline and approvals you need before starting. Use the intake sheet if helpful. Ask questions about gaps. Never guess an address, product size, testimonial or offer.','Help me collect inputs for [chosen service]. Agreed package: [files]. What I already have: [facts and assets]. Ask only the missing questions. Make a plain checklist for audience, goal, approved facts, files/rights, next-step link, deadline and who approves the work. Do not ask for passwords or unnecessary personal information.','A completed client-input sheet.','Essential facts and assets are available, or gaps are explicitly agreed.'),
 step('Make the first version','Use your chosen service’s click-by-click recipe below. Start with one page, clip, listing or row. Review that small piece before producing the full package. Keep editable originals and name files clearly. For websites, publish a tested static preview. For stores, use a proper storefront instead of pretending an HTML page processes orders.','Make the first [page/clip/design/listing/workflow] for [service] from these approved inputs [facts]. Give exact creation steps, a complete small draft and file naming. Mark missing information. Then provide a test checklist. Keep the starter scope and avoid unsupported claims, fake functionality or copied competitor work.','One working first piece and its editable original.','The piece matches the agreed facts and does the job it is supposed to do.'),
 step('Finish the agreed package','Once the first piece works, make the remaining files using the same standards. Do not add extra features without agreement. Keep approved wording and styles consistent. Use the chosen-service check below on each file.','Review my agreed file list [scope] against what I made [files and notes]. Identify missing or inconsistent pieces. Give an ordered finishing checklist for [service]. Do not expand the scope. Flag where owner approval is still needed.','Every agreed file, named and organized.','The package matches the agreement without missing pieces or unpriced extra work.'),
 step('Test it as the client would use it','Open exports on a phone and computer. Tap links, test a fake enquiry, read captions, check formulas or play clips according to your service. Compare facts with the approved input sheet. Ask the approver to check the intended message. Fix errors before delivery.','Create a final test plan for [service], [files] and [agreed purpose]. Include factual checks, links, mobile reading, permissions and any service-specific functional test. Show expected result for each test. Flag assumptions instead of declaring success without checking.','A passed final checklist and any agreed limits.','A new user can open and use the files without a broken link or unexplained step.'),
 step('Send the files and explain how to use them','“Delivery” means sending the final files plus simple use instructions. Put final exports and editable files in separate folders. Share access only with the right people. Send a short email naming the files, what to do first and how to request the agreed revision. For a site or form, use the client’s account and agree ongoing costs.','Write a delivery message for [service] using [file list], [links], [first action], [revision terms] and [agreed ongoing costs]. Explain each file in one sentence. Ask for approval by [agreed date]. Do not claim a client result. Include a check that permissions and downloads work.','Accessible final files and a plain delivery message.','The client knows which file to open first and can actually access it.'),
 step('Collect approval and review your time','Log requested changes against the agreed revision limit. Correct mistakes and confirm approval. Record time and costs. Ask whether you may show the work or quote feedback. Only use permissioned, accurate proof. Choose one process improvement for the next project.','Use this feedback [actual reply] and agreement [scope/revisions] to draft my next response. Separate corrections from new scope. Ask for approval after the agreed changes. Then summarize actual hours, costs and one improvement. Ask permission before suggesting any public portfolio use.','Approval, a final version and a short project review.','Feedback is real and any public use has permission.')],EX,FIX,['A','G','D'])
book('G','copywriting-project','Write clear words for your offer','both','Write the words people need to understand your service, download or affiliate demonstration. Copywriting service sellers can also use this for client work.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Collect the facts before writing','Write reader, offer, exact contents, approved facts, price if known and the next action. Open the relevant page or product record. Explain unfamiliar terms. For affiliate content use the real seller’s specifications and your own test.','Organize these facts [inputs] into reader, problem, exact offer, evidence, limits, price and next action. Ask for missing essentials. Separate facts from assumptions. Do not invent benefits, social proof or specifications.','A fact sheet for the words you will write.','Every important claim has a fact or is left as a question.'),
 step('Write the headline and short explanation','Say what it is, who it helps and the task it supports. Then list the exact files or outcome. A website package is one tested page. A planner helps organize a week. A product stand supports the compatible phone you actually tested. Avoid “unlock your potential” language.','Write five plain headlines and a 60-word explanation for [offer] and [reader] using [facts]. Name the concrete task and exact contents. Give three factual benefits and one limitation. No invented results, jargon or dramatic promises.','One clear headline and short offer explanation.','The reader can explain what you sell after one read.'),
 step('Answer the questions that block a decision','Use actual questions where possible. Explain what is included, how it works, required tools, access/delivery, revisions or refunds and who it is not suitable for. Do not manufacture policy terms.','Write six FAQs from [actual questions] for [offer], using [verified contents and real terms]. Keep each answer under sixty words. Explain tools, delivery/access, limits, support and next steps. Mark unanswered policy questions. No fake guarantees.','Six useful FAQ answers.','Answers match the actual package and current terms.'),
 step('Write the next-step messages','Write one preview message and one response to an interested person. For clients, ask permission to share the sample. For downloads, deliver the preview and explain the first task. For affiliates, use a disclosed approved link and answer fit questions honestly.','Write a short preview message and an interested-buyer reply for [offer], [channel], [actual sample/link] and [facts]. Give the promised resource first and one next question. Include affiliate disclosure if relevant. No auto sending, pressure, fake scarcity or unsupported outcome claims.','Two ready-to-review messages.','The resource is delivered before the pitch and the link works.'),
 step('Edit for clarity and accuracy','Read every line aloud. Replace abstract words with the exact file, task or click. Remove claims you cannot prove. Ask someone unfamiliar with the offer what they would receive. Fix their confusion.','Edit this text [draft] for a complete beginner. Replace jargon with plain words. Keep verified facts unchanged. List unsupported claims, missing details and confusing next steps. Give the final concise version and a fact-check table.','A clear checked page or message.','No invented proof or unexplained term remains.')],{'title':'A small copy project','body':'This text file shows a headline and three emails using fictional approved facts. Read it as writing practice. It does not represent customer results.','asset':'copy-project.txt'},FIX,['A','B','I'])
book('H','ai-video-creative','Use AI visuals only where they help','both','Make one optional visual insert with a storyboard and a realistic credit budget. You can skip generated visuals and use your actual screen or product.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Choose the visual’s job','Decide what the visual explains. For a website seller, the actual page is stronger proof than an imagined office. For a planner, show the sheet. For physical goods, show the real item. AI or stock can set context, but must not pretend to show a customer or product test.','My offer is [offer]. Actual footage/output: [files]. Suggest one optional illustrative visual and explain its job. Prefer the real screen or product when it proves the claim. Give a zero-subscription alternative. Do not invent customers, testimonials or product performance.','One reasoned visual choice or a decision to skip generation.','The visual adds clarity without replacing real proof.'),
 step('Make a short shot plan','Write scene, length, what stays consistent and where it fits in the edit. Keep the first test to one short insert. For a stock route open Pexels, search the specific action, keep the source URL and check the license.','Plan one 3–5 second illustrative shot for [purpose], using [approved assets]. Give subject, action, camera, lighting and what must remain consistent. Add a real-footage or Pexels alternative and an asset-rights check. No branded product distortion or fake testimonial.','One shot plan with a free alternative.','You know what the shot explains and how you may use the assets.'),
 step('Check the plan and generation cost','Open the current provider’s plan and the Generate cost for the selected model, duration and resolution. Use the calculator in Tools & costs. Budget retries and failed attempts. Do not divide credits by one generation and call that finished videos.','Use these displayed plan facts [price, credits, model, duration, resolution, cost/attempt]. Estimate [shots] generated shots per finished edit and [attempts] attempts per usable shot. Show assumptions, credits per edit and approximate capacity. Include other costs. No fixed output promise.','A budget with checked current inputs.','The estimate includes retries and is labeled as an estimate.'),
 step('Generate or capture one test and inspect it','Use the prompt, select the model/cost you checked and generate one test. Inspect hands, text, product shape, motion and continuity. If it fails, change one part of the prompt or use your free footage. Never fabricate a before/after product result.','Refine this visual prompt [prompt] based on the actual problem [what failed]. Keep [approved product/person/setting facts] consistent. Change only the needed part. Suggest when to stop retrying and use real footage instead. Do not imply generated footage is a real test.','One accepted illustrative clip or free replacement.','The clip has no misleading detail or obvious artifact.'),
 step('Edit it into the real demonstration','Add the insert to your vertical project. Keep the actual output visible and let the voice explain the real process. Add disclosure when platform rules or the content require it. Export and check on a phone. Log the actual credits used.','Review this edit plan [timeline] containing an illustrative generated clip. Check whether a viewer could mistake it for a real customer or product test. Give clearer wording or placement, caption corrections and a final export checklist. Keep actual evidence as the main demonstration.','One checked edit and actual cost log.','The real result remains clear and the visual does not mislead.')],{'title':'A short product storyboard','body':'The storyboard uses a fictional desk organizer as an editing exercise. Generated inserts are optional. The actual item must be filmed for a real review.','asset':'video-storyboard.csv'},FIX,['C','E','D'])
book('I','lead-follow-up','Help an interested person take the next step','both','Deliver the resource you promised, answer the real question and show a relevant next action without pressure.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Prepare a preview that works','For services, prepare one small sample. For downloads, prepare one useful excerpt and explain what the full file adds. For affiliates, prepare a disclosed real-item demonstration. Open the link in a private browser window and check permissions.','Prepare a preview-delivery checklist for [offer], [actual sample], [link] and [channel]. Explain what the person can do with it, what is excluded and which permissions to test. No invented customer proof or unsolicited bulk messages.','A working preview and short use instruction.','The person can open the promised resource without asking for access.'),
 step('Send what you promised first','If someone asks for the resource, send it with one sentence explaining what to do. Ask which part of the task they are working on. Do not hide the preview behind an immediate hard pitch. Reply only through an appropriate permitted channel.','Write a reply to [actual request]. Deliver [resource link] first. Explain the first useful action in one sentence. Ask one relevant question about [task]. Keep the tone friendly and concise. No pressure, fake urgency or automatic sending.','A reviewed preview reply.','The reply fulfills the original promise.'),
 step('Match the offer to their actual question','Read their response. For a client, ask about scope. For a download, show the relevant page or worksheet. For an affiliate item, answer fit using checked facts and disclose commission. If it does not fit, say so.','Use this actual reply [message] and verified offer facts [facts]. Draft a helpful response that answers the question and suggests one relevant next action only if the offer fits. Identify missing information and reasons not to recommend it. Include affiliate disclosure if relevant.','One helpful buyer-specific response.','The answer addresses what they asked instead of repeating a generic pitch.'),
 step('Log what happened','Record preview request, resource sent, response, quote or product click, and confirmed purchase separately. Keep notes short and factual. Don’t store unnecessary personal information. Follow up only when context and rules allow, then stop after refusal or an unanswered final check.','Organize these real conversation notes [notes] into requested resource, sent date, actual question, next agreed action and outcome. Separate interest from purchase and pending from available funds. Draft one optional respectful check-in where appropriate. Do not invent responses.','A factual tracker row and agreed next step.','The record distinguishes a conversation from a paid order.'),
 step('Improve one confusing part','Review repeated questions. Is the preview unclear, the offer too broad, the link broken or the price unexplained? Change one thing and test again. If someone bought, help them use the files and collect real feedback with permission.','Review [actual questions and outcomes] for [offer]. Find one confusing part of preview, page, scope or delivery. Give a specific change and a small test. Do not assume all silence means a price problem. No guaranteed conversion improvement.','One tested improvement.','The change comes from actual feedback and the next test is clear.')],{'title':'Reply examples for different offers','body':'These messages deliver a resource first, then ask a relevant question. Replace the links and facts with your actual offer. They are drafts to review and send manually.','asset':'lead-replies.txt'},FIX,['G','D','C'])
book('J','affiliate-product-demo','Check eligibility and demonstrate an affiliate item','product','For the three physical-product niches, check the program first, inspect a real item and make a truthful demonstration. Download sellers can skip this guide.',FREE,PAID,[
 step('Check whether the program accepts you','Open the affiliate program’s official rules for your country. Check age, location, account requirements, payment details, product/category rules and any limits. US TikTok Shop rules are not global. If your country/account is not eligible, choose an accepting program or return to a download/service. Do not borrow another person’s country or account.','Help me make an eligibility checklist for [program], [country], [age category], [account status]. Use current official sources I provide [links]. Separate verified rules, unknowns and next checks. Do not assume US TikTok Shop rules apply globally. If not eligible, suggest an eligible download or service route without account/location workarounds.','A verified eligibility decision and payout check.','You are accepted or have chosen another viable route before spending on samples.'),
 step('Choose one item using evidence','Use the selected niche below. Compare three actual items by fit, price, seller reputation, recent reviews, shipping, returns, commission and sample cost. Category research explains why a niche is worth testing, but it does not prove a particular item sells. Save current source links and dates.','Compare these three actual listings [URLs and verified facts] for [buyer task]. Return fit, dimensions/materials, seller/shipping/returns, recent review concerns, commission, sample cost and unknowns. Separate category research from item demand. Do not invent sales counts or star ratings. Recommend an inspection candidate, not guaranteed profit.','One item to inspect and a comparison sheet.','The item solves a specific task and all important details are checked.'),
 step('Inspect a real sample','Obtain the actual item through an allowed route. Check package contents, dimensions, compatibility and the practical use you will show. Photograph or film the real product. If it disappoints, don’t recommend it. Free stock and AI cannot establish product performance.','Create an inspection plan for [actual item], [seller specifications] and [buyer task]. Give exact checks, camera shots and limits. Use only claims that can be verified from the real product or supplied label. No health, safety, durability or income claims beyond evidence.','A real-item inspection record and footage.','The product matches the link and the demonstrated use.'),
 step('Record a useful disclosed demo','Film what arrives, what problem it is for, how to use it, the actual result and one limitation. Use C’s timing recipe. State the affiliate relationship clearly. Use your own footage or authorized seller assets without pretending they are your test.','Write a 30-second real-item demo from [inspection findings]. Give exact shots, voiceover, on-screen text, one limitation and clear affiliate disclosure. Link only [approved product URL]. Do not invent a personal experience, result or comparison.','One factual demonstration and approved link.','The viewer sees the real item, knows the limitation and understands you may earn commission.'),
 step('Track confirmed commission and payout separately','Use the program dashboard to record orders, returns, approved commission, settlement and available withdrawal. Rates and rules vary. A click or pending order is not money in your bank. Review actual buyer questions before testing another item.','Organize these actual program records [orders, returns, commission, settlement status] into pending, approved and available amounts. Include known fees and sample costs. Identify missing payout rules and official checks. Suggest one content improvement from [buyer questions]. No estimated income from views alone.','A commission record and a sensible next test.','Only actual approved/available amounts are counted as such.')],{'title':'Affiliate checks before filming','body':'This worksheet helps you verify country, account, item and payout rules. Fill it with the program’s actual requirements. An empty field means you still need to check.','asset':'affiliate-checklist.txt'},FIX,['C','E','D'])
assert len(BOOKS)==10
